{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774493905,"nonce":"3pqc6qIWasrlPJkL1OEi1c48WAg=","title":"Convention: always redirect agent/subprocess output to files, never rely on shell stdout","message":"Agent-spawned background processes (codex exec, gemini, long-running shell commands) must ALWAYS redirect output to a file, never rely on capturing stdout from a backgrounded shell process. If the shell dies, the output is lost.\n\nExample of the failure: `codex exec ... \u0026` launched in background — process finished but output went to the shell's stdout which was not captured. The analysis was lost and had to be re-run.\n\nCorrect pattern:\n```bash\ncodex exec ... \u003e /tmp/codex_output.json 2\u003e\u00261 \u0026\n```\n\nOr use the Agent tool / Bash tool with run_in_background which automatically captures output.\n\nThis should be a hard convention: no background process without explicit output redirection to a persistent file. The file path should be logged so it can be retrieved later.\n\nSession: 31fa1977","files":null}]}